h1: client -> server carries
	note=ClientHello + key_share
	message=sync
	description=The client guesses the key exchange group and sends its public key share now, so no second round trip is needed. It also sends supported versions, cipher suites, the server name (SNI) and the protocols it speaks (ALPN).
	example=key_share X25519, SNI example.com, ALPN h2 and http/1.1.
h2: server -> client produces
	note=ServerHello + key_share
	message=reply
	description=The server picks the cipher suite and sends its own key share. From here on, both sides can compute the same shared secret.
	example=TLS_AES_128_GCM_SHA256, key_share X25519.
h3: client -> key-schedule produces
	note=derive handshake keys
	message=sync
	description=The client combines its private key with the server's share, then derives the handshake traffic secrets from that shared secret and the transcript of both hellos.
h4: key-schedule -> client produces
	note=client and server handshake keys
	message=reply
h5: server -> client carries
	note={EncryptedExtensions}
	message=async
	description=Encrypted from here on. It carries the server's answers to the client's extensions.
	example=ALPN h2.
h6: server -> client carries
	note={Certificate}
	message=async
	description=The server's certificate chain, encrypted, so an observer cannot see which certificate the server presented.
h7: server -> client carries
	note={CertificateVerify}
	message=async
	description=A signature over the transcript with the certificate's private key. It proves the server holds that key now, not only that it once got the certificate.
h8: server -> client carries
	note={Finished}
	message=async
	description=A MAC over the whole transcript. Any change to an earlier message by an attacker makes it fail.
h9: client -> trust-store checks
	note=verify chain to a trusted root
	message=sync
	description=The client checks each signature in the chain, the validity dates, the name example.com, and revocation where it checks it.
h10: trust-store -> client produces
	note=trusted for example.com
	message=reply
h11: client -> server carries
	note={Finished}
	message=async
	description=The client's MAC over the transcript. The handshake is complete for the client after one round trip.
h12: client -> key-schedule produces
	note=derive application keys
	message=sync
	description=The application traffic secrets come from the master secret and the transcript through the server's Finished.
h13: key-schedule -> client produces
	note=client and server application keys
	message=reply
h14: client -> server carries
	note=[GET /pricing]
	message=async
	description=The first HTTP request, encrypted with application keys, leaves with or right after the client's Finished.
	example=This is where the page load's HTTP request step begins.
h15: client -> server carries
	note=[early data] on resumption
	message=async
	fragment=opt:resumed session with 0-RTT
	description=A client that resumes a session with a pre-shared key may send early data in its first flight. Early data can be replayed by an attacker, so a server accepts it only for requests that are safe to repeat.
